  "summary": "The tawny-flanked prinia (Prinia subflava) is a small passerine bird belonging to the genus Prinia in the family Cisticolidae, a family of Old World warblers. It is widespread and common in most parts of Africa south of the Sahara. The plain prinia (P. inornata) of southern Asia was formerly included in this species but is now usually considered to be a separate species.",
  "appearance": {
    "description": "The tawny-flanked prinia is 11–12 cm (4.3–4.7 in) in length with a long, narrow, graduated tail and a fairly long, slender bill. The tail is often held erect or waved from side to side. The upperparts are grey-brown with rufous-brown edges to the flight feathers and a rufous tinge to the rump.",
    "colors": "The plain prinia (P. inornata) of southern Asia was formerly included in this species but is now usually considered to be a separate species. The upperparts are grey-brown with rufous-brown edges to the flight feathers and a rufous tinge to the rump.",
